@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 8: linear algebra)

Matrices are the native type, so the standard decompositions are one call each.

@section(Solving systems)

Use the backslash for `A x = b`: it picks the right method and is more accurate than `inv(A) * b`.

@octo
A = [4 1 2; 1 5 3; 2 3 6]; b = [1; 2; 3];
x = A \ b
norm(A * x - b) < 1e-12
linsolve(A, b)'
inv(A) * b - x
pinv([1 2; 2 4]) * [1; 2]
@end

@section(Matrix properties)

These numbers tell you whether a system is well posed.

@octo
A = [4 1 2; 1 5 3; 2 3 6];
det(A), rank(A), trace(A), cond(A)
norm(A), norm(A, 1), norm(A, "fro"), norm([3 4])
null([1 1; 1 1])', orth([1 1; 1 1])'
dot([1 2 3], [4 5 6]), cross([1 0 0], [0 1 0]), kron([1 2], [1; 1])
issymmetric(A), isdiag(eye(2)), istriu(triu(A)), tril(A)
issquare(A), arg(1i)
@end

@section(Decompositions)

Each factorisation answers a different question: LU for repeated solves, QR for least squares, Cholesky for symmetric positive matrices, eig and SVD for structure.

@octo
A = [4 1 2; 1 5 3; 2 3 6];
[L, U, P] = lu(A); norm(P * A - L * U) < 1e-12
[Q, R] = qr(A); norm(Q * R - A) < 1e-12
R = chol(A); norm(R' * R - A) < 1e-12
[V, D] = eig(A); diag(D)'
svd(A)'
[U, S, W] = svd([1 2; 3 4; 5 6]); diag(S)'
[T, Sc] = schur(A); size(Sc)
@end

@section(Matrix functions)

`expm`, `sqrtm` and `logm` are matrix functions, not element-wise ones.

@octo
expm(zeros(2))
B = sqrtm([4 0; 0 9])
logm(expm([1 0; 0 2]))
A = [2 1; 1 3]; A^2, A.^2
mldivide([2 0; 0 4], [2; 4])', mrdivide([2 4], [2 0; 0 4]), power(2, 10)
@end

@section(Least squares and sparse matrices)

An overdetermined system solved with backslash gives the least-squares fit; sparse matrices store only the non-zeros.

@octo
x = (1:6)'; y = [2.1 3.9 6.2 7.8 10.1 12.2]';
coef = [ones(6, 1) x] \ y
S = sparse([1 2 3], [1 2 3], [5 6 7]);
issparse(S), nnz(S), full(S)
T = spdiags([-1 2 -1] .* ones(5, 1), -1:1, 5, 5); full(T)(1:2, :)
speye(3) * [1; 2; 3]
@end
